      "writtenOn": "2023-08-10T21:10:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "While it seems we may not want a `std::any` style `any_cast`, `std::optional` uses `value` and `value_or` (which return references) because the idea is to model value semantics as opposed to pointer semantics (like `std::unique_ptr::get` which returns a pointer). Not sure if it makes a difference here. I suppose access has to go through `operator-\u003e` which looks like a pointer, so might as well just do that as opposed to `std::optional` which looks a bit confused on that issue.\n\nOn the other hand, an `any_cast` here could at least statically check that the type being cast to derives from `Base`, which would be useful if ever refactoring things in the future.",
      "range": {
